{
  "source_pdf": "synthetic",
  "model": "synthetic fixture (hand-written)",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "[SYNTHETIC FIXTURE] Dissociative adsorption of formic acid on anatase TiO2 nanoparticles",
    "authors": [],
    "doi": null,
    "year": null,
    "summary": "Synthetic fixture for pipeline testing, not a real publication: it identifies the adsorption mode of formic acid on anatase TiO2 nanoparticles (~20 nm, predominantly (101) facets) at room temperature. XRD shows phase-pure anatase without reported lattice parameters, TEM shows truncated bipyramids dominated by (101) facets, DRIFTS shows asymmetric and symmetric OCO stretches at 1560 and 1360 cm-1 and no C=O band near 1700 cm-1, and O 1s XPS shows a new component at 531.6 eV. The authors interpret the 200 cm-1 OCO splitting as bridging bidentate formate over two five-fold coordinated Ti atoms, and the new O 1s component as a surface hydroxyl formed when the acidic proton transfers to a neighbouring two-fold coordinated bridging oxygen. The reconstruction target is formate on anatase TiO2 (101); the formate coverage and the position of the dissociated proton are unresolved."
  },
